WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Rare FNIP gene variants tied to less abdominal fat, greater muscle mass, and sharply lower

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:13.800
cardiometabolic disease risk. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 143. Analysis of more than

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:21.300
1 million people from diverse ancestries identified uncommon variants in FNIP genes. Carriers showed

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:27.600
reduced abdominal fat, increased muscle, and a 60% lower risk of cardiometabolic conditions,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.500
along with protection from obesity. The variants appear to alter fat distribution and muscle

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.500
maintenance through mechanisms still under study. Two companion papers in nature detail how these

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:46.120
rare changes correlate with body composition differences across ancestry groups. This work adds to

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:51.820
ongoing efforts to map genetic factors that influence body composition and disease susceptibility.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.160
Follow-up studies will test whether these protective effects hold in additional populations

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:05.040
and whether the pathway can be modulated safely. The nature study of rare FNIP variants provides direct

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.540
genetic evidence that altered fat distribution and muscle maintenance can coincide with substantial

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:16.840
cardiometabolic protection. Carriers of these variants offer a natural model for dissecting how

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:23.780
specific tissues influence whole body metabolic risk. Because the variants are uncommon, the immediate

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:29.980
clinical translation path remains unclear, yet they sharpen questions about which downstream pathways

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:36.740
could be safely targeted. The work also supplies new benchmarks for polygenic risk scores that incorporate

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:42.960
body composition traits. What functional experiments would best reveal whether mimicking these variants

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:49.760
could produce similar protection in people without the protective alleles? Now, shifting to a very

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.740
different area of research. A study of adults around age 55 found that normal blood pressure,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:02.000
absence of diabetes, and never smoking were each associated with up to 13 additional years of

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.640
dementia-free survival. The neurology paper quantifies how these three factors together extend the

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:15.160
period without cognitive impairment. The analysis drew on longitudinal health records to isolate the

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:21.200
contribution of each factor while controlling for other variables. Next steps include testing whether

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.560
interventions that improve these same factors shift dementia incidents in randomized settings.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:34.740
On a different note, researchers tracked 1,154 hospitalized patients across 20 centers and analyzed

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:41.960
more than 200,000 samples. Severe COVID was associated with persistent reactivation of over 10

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:48.900
latent viruses in the first 40 days after admission. Long COVID cases showed specific reactivation

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:55.360
of anelo very day. The findings point to broad immune disruption that allows latent viruses to

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:01.380
re-emerge. The study authors note that the reactivation pattern differed between patients who recovered

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:07.860
and those who developed prolonged symptoms. On-going work will examine whether early antiviral or

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:14.780
immune-modulating interventions reduce these reactivations. Now, moving from modern immune challenges

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:21.580
to an ancient immune strategy discovered in flatworms. Investigators identified glandular cells that

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:27.580
undergo rapid cell death and release cytotoxic agents capable of killing nearby cells and bacteria

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:34.080
within minutes. The work describes a previously unrecognized immune strategy in these regenerative

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:40.560
flatworms. The mechanism offers a model for studying rapid localized immune responses without

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:47.620
traditional inflammatory pathways. The cells, termed ruptoblasts, appear concentrated in specific

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:53.560
tissues that encounter pathogens during feeding or movement. Researchers plan to map the molecular

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:59.100
triggers that initiate this explosive release. Now, here's something most people get wrong.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:06.120
Most people assume every organism on Earth descends from a single origin event. Protein structure

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:12.820
comparisons now indicate that bacteria and archaea independently evolved distinct enzymes to perform

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:18.980
the same core metabolic reactions. Right now, the enzymes inside your cells reflect one of those

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:24.740
ancient solutions, while microbes and other domains reflect the other. The analysis examined

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:31.200
genomes across both domains and found structurally unrelated proteins catalyzing identical essential

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.260
steps. One memorable detail is that these parallel inventions occurred for reactions considered

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:44.040
indispensable to early metabolism. The finding forces are re-examination of how metabolic networks

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:50.020
first stabilized and whether environmental conditions on early Earth permitted more than one successful

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:55.920
starting configuration. Future missions searching for life elsewhere may therefore need to consider

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:02.480
whether multiple independent origins are statistically plausible rather than assuming a single starting point.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:08.860
From unconventional immune mechanisms to unconventional ideas about the origin of life itself,

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:15.460
research guided by the double empathy problem theory indicates that mismatches in social cues and

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.760
expectations occur in both directions rather than reflecting a one-sided empathy deficit.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.680
The findings challenge earlier assumptions that place the communication burden solely on autistic

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:32.860
individuals. Studies continue to examine how environments and interaction styles can reduce these

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:39.280
mutual misunderstandings. The review synthesizes behavioral experiments showing that non-autistic

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.660
participants also struggle to interpret autistic communication patterns accurately. Additional work

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:52.140
is testing training approaches that improve bidirectional understanding. From social perception to deep

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:59.640
time perception of early animal movement, a site in Canada yielded fossils dated to 567 million

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:05.760
years ago that show evidence of movement, sexual reproduction, and recognizable body plans.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:12.280
The discovery advances the timeline for these traits by up to 10 million years and suggests complex

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:18.740
animal life may have first appeared in deeper ocean settings. Paleontologists are now comparing the

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:25.740
specimens with other etiocharan assemblages to refine evolutionary sequences. The fossils preserve

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:31.520
impressions of organisms that could move across the seafloor and display differentiated body regions.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.780
Continued excavation aims to recover additional specimens that clarify reproductive structures.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:44.460
From ancient body plans to ancient metabolic inventions, comparison of enzyme structures across

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:50.400
bacterial and archaeal genomes revealed cases where the two domains independently evolved distinct

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:57.200
enzymes to catalyze identical essential metabolic reactions. The pattern implies separate evolutionary

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:04.260
starting points rather than a single common origin for all life. The analysis draws on genomic data

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:10.520
from both domains to identify the structural differences. Researchers examined hundreds of enzyme

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:16.740
families and found multiple instances of structurally unrelated proteins performing the same reactions.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:22.440
Further genome and fossil work will test how early metabolic systems diverged.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:28.480
New measurements and genetic findings continue to refine how we understand both present day biology

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:35.660
and its deep history. Keep an eye on further functional experiments that test whether FNIP pathway mimicry

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:41.160
can be achieved safely in broader populations. That's planetarian daily for today.

